</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Memorization Audits</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we explore the memory capacity of LLMs.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.1. </span>FinLake-Bench</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">FinLeak-Bench comprises 2,000 financial question-answer pairs spanning January 2022 to June 2023, covering four categories: price inquiries (e.g., NVIDIA’s closing price on a specific date), trend predictions (e.g., Apple’s stock movement over two months), event impacts (e.g., Tesla’s stock response to major corporate announcements), and market performance (e.g., top-performing NASDAQ-100 stocks on a given date). Representative examples and their corresponding scoring criteria are summarized in Table <a href="#S2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 2.2.3. Results and Insights ‣ 2.2. Counterfactual Evaluation ‣ 2. Is Information Leakage Everywhere? ‣ Profit Mirage: Revisiting Information Leakage in LLM-based Financial Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.2. </span>Baselines and metrics</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate three leading LLMs—GPT-4o, Claude-Sonnet-3.7<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2024</a>)</cite>, and Grok-3<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">xAI, 2024</a>)</cite>—using a strict accuracy metric:</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{Accuracy}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{I}\left[\hat{a}_{i}=a_{i}\right]\cdot w_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Accuracy</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Accuracy}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{I}\left[\hat{a}_{i}=a_{i}\right]\cdot w_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the predicted and true answers for question <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of questions, and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a weight reflecting answer precision. Price inquiries score 1 point for answers within ±1% error and 0.5 for ±3%. Trend predictions earn 0.5 points for correct direction and 0.5 for accurate magnitude. Event impact questions are scored based on qualitative alignment with actual market reactions. Market performance questions score 1 point for identifying stocks in the top/bottom 3% and 0.5 for top/bottom 5%.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.3. </span>Results and Insights</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.3.3. Results and Insights ‣ 2.3. Memorization Audits ‣ 2. Is Information Leakage Everywhere? ‣ Profit Mirage: Revisiting Information Leakage in LLM-based Financial Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents the average accuracy of LLMs on FinLeak-Bench, revealing significant memorization. All models exhibit high average accuracy across question types, ranging from 85.37% for price inquiries to 92.94% for event impacts. Event impact questions show the highest accuracy, indicating near-encyclopedic recall of market reactions to specific events. Price inquiries (85.37%) and market performance (89.83%) accuracies suggest precise memory of individual data points and relative rankings. Most critically, the 90.23% accuracy on trend prediction questions, which require recalling temporal sequences, confirms that models memorize complete market movement patterns, enabling them to “predict” historical outcomes with high fidelity. This memorization directly contributes to information leakage in financial forecasting, as models rely on recalled patterns rather than input-driven reasoning, undermining generalization.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.07920v1/2.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:200/97;" width="200" height="97" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Memorization audits of LLMs on FinLeak-Bench</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4. </span>Before-and-after Fine-tuning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we assess the extent to which the acquired knowledge via training affects the performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.07920v1/3.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/93;" width="229" height="93" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><span id="S2.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Before-and-after fine-tuning on model behavior.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.4.1. </span>Experimental Setting</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We fine-tune two base models, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2024</a>)</cite> and Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta AI, 2024</a>)</cite>, using LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2021</a>)</cite> (rank=64, alpha=16, dropout=0.1, batch size=16, gradient accumulation steps=4, learning rate=2e-4, warmup ratio=0.03) on the FNSPID dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, comprising Dow Jones Industrial Average (DJIA) constituent stocks (30 stocks) from January 2020 to December 2022. The test set includes market data for these DJIA stocks from January to June 2022.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.4.2. </span>Baselines and metrics</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the impact of fine-tuning using three metrics. The Bias Score<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> quantifies prediction bias toward frequently observed stocks:</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Bias}=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S}\frac{f_{\text{train}}(s)\cdot p_{\text{score}}(s)}{\sum_{s^{\prime}=1}^{S}f_{\text{train}}(s^{\prime})}-\frac{1}{S}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Bias</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>S</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>score</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>S</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Bias}=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S}\frac{f_{\text{train}}(s)\cdot p_{\text{score}}(s)}{\sum_{s^{\prime}=1}^{S}f_{\text{train}}(s^{\prime})}-\frac{1}{S},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{train}}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{train}}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> is the frequency of stock <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> in the training data, <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{score}}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>score</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{score}}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> is the model’s prediction score for stock <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of stocks. The Memory Score measures alignment with historical patterns:</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Memory}=\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\cos\left(\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{model}},\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{hist}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Memory</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>hist</mtext></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Memory}=\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\cos\left(\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{model}},\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{hist}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{model}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{model}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{hist}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>hist</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{hist}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the model’s prediction and historical pattern vectors at time <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of time points. The Generalization Change<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hushchyn and Ustyuzhanin, 2021</a>)</cite> assesses the relative change in accuracy on unseen data:</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\text{Gen}}=\frac{\text{Acc}_{\text{unseen}}^{\text{post}}-\text{Acc}_{\text{unseen}}^{\text{pre}}}{\text{Acc}_{\text{unseen}}^{\text{pre}}}\times 100\%," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>Gen</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Acc</mtext><mtext>unseen</mtext><mtext>post</mtext></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mtext>Acc</mtext><mtext>unseen</mtext><mtext>pre</mtext></msubsup></mrow><msubsup><mtext>Acc</mtext><mtext>unseen</mtext><mtext>pre</mtext></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\text{Gen}}=\frac{\text{Acc}_{\text{unseen}}^{\text{post}}-\text{Acc}_{\text{unseen}}^{\text{pre}}}{\text{Acc}_{\text{unseen}}^{\text{pre}}}\times 100\%,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Acc}_{\text{unseen}}^{\text{pre}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>Acc</mtext><mtext>unseen</mtext><mtext>pre</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Acc}_{\text{unseen}}^{\text{pre}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Acc}_{\text{unseen}}^{\text{post}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>Acc</mtext><mtext>unseen</mtext><mtext>post</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Acc}_{\text{unseen}}^{\text{post}}</annotation></semantics></math> are pre-fine-tuning and post-fine-tuning accuracies on unseen data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.4.3. </span>Results and Insights</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.4. Before-and-after Fine-tuning ‣ 2. Is Information Leakage Everywhere? ‣ Profit Mirage: Revisiting Information Leakage in LLM-based Financial Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> illustrates the impact of fine-tuning on model behavior. Training markedly improves in-distribution accuracy, with Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct increasing from 51.61% to 72.16% and Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct from 54.73% to 76.52%. However, this performance leap is accompanied by increased prediction bias, with Bias Scores of 0.2895 and 0.3122, indicating that models no longer treat all stocks impartially, favoring frequently observed ones. Moreover, High Memory Scores of 0.7759 and 0.8113 show that models closely replicate historical patterns from the training data, suggesting they memorize future outcomes rather than learning robust forecasting principles. Critically, generalization capability declines substantially, with reductions of 21.53% and 18.06% in accuracy on unseen data, confirming that fine-tuning leads to overfitting on leaked data. This behavior, driven by training data, exacerbates information leakage risks as models reproduce memorized patterns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These findings highlight that fine-tuned models are not smarter but rather memorize historical patterns!</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Our Approach: A Counterfactual Evolution Framework</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Preliminaries</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To escape the mirage issues, we propose <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FactFin</span>, an external framework that mitigates leakage by using LLMs as strategy generators rather than direct decision-makers, leveraging counterfactual reasoning and strategy evolution. We define the market state at time <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}=\{P_{t},F_{t},N_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}=\{P_{t},F_{t},N_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is price data, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes market factors, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> represents factorized news. A trading strategy <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:S_{t}\to A_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:S_{t}\to A_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to actions <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{t}\in\{\text{buy},\text{sell},\text{hold}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>buy</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>sell</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>hold</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{t}\in\{\text{buy},\text{sell},\text{hold}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, LLM-based Agents are forced to learn why an outcome occurred, because the outcome itself is no longer fixed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Framework Overview</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As depicted in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.4. RAG for Market Factors ‣ 3. Our Approach: A Counterfactual Evolution Framework ‣ Profit Mirage: Revisiting Information Leakage in LLM-based Financial Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, our <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FactFin</span> is an external framework that mitigates information leakage in LLM-based financial prediction by using LLMs as strategy generators, coupled with counterfactual reasoning and strategy evolution. FactFin integrates four core components: Strategy Code Generator (SCG), Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS), and Counterfactual Simulator (CS). These components synergistically form a pipeline that generates and optimizes trading strategies driven by real-time market inputs, ensuring robust predictions for LLM-based financial forecasting methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Strategy Code Generator</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Strategy Code Generator (SCG) transforms financial prediction into a code generation task, leveraging LLMs to produce executable trading strategy code based on market state <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. SCG generates a strategy <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:S_{t}\to A_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:S_{t}\to A_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\text{SCG}(S_{t},P)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>SCG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\text{SCG}(S_{t},P),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a prompt template. By focusing on generating systematic strategies rather than specific predictions, we reduce the model’s reliance on memorized historical price movements.
A simplified prompt is as follows<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            The templates will change based on different market conditions, fully disclosed in the Appendix.</span></span></span>:</p>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.SS3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle" style="--ltx-border-color:#000000;">
<span id="S3.SS3.p1.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:327.8pt;--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">
<span id="S3.SS3.p1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">[Prompt Template]</span>
<span id="S3.SS3.p1.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_p">Given market state with {Prices}, {Factors}, and {News}, generate executable trading strategy code, e.g., {Examples}, using only provided inputs.</span>
</span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>RAG for Market Factors</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al., 2020</a>)</cite> component enhances the Strategy Code Generator by retrieving and processing real-time market factors within <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring strategies rely on current inputs rather than memorized data. RAG transforms the market state <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}=\{P_{t},F_{t},N_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}=\{P_{t},F_{t},N_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> into structured features <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}^{\prime}=\text{RAG}(S_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>RAG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}^{\prime}=\text{RAG}(S_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> includes processed prices, factors, and news. For news data, RAG extracts quantified features:</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}^{\prime}=\psi(N_{t},t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}^{\prime}=\psi(N_{t},t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> converts news at time <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> into sentiment scores and topic distributions in <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. This approach "factorizes" unstructured information, making it more suitable for systematic trading strategies while reducing the risk of the model falling back on memorized outcomes.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.07920v1/4.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:234/210;" width="234" height="210" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>. </span><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The overall architecture of our FactFin. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5. </span>MCTS for Strategy Evolution</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Silver et al., 2016</a>)</cite> component optimizes strategies generated by the SCG, ensuring adaptation to market factors in <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> while avoiding reliance on memorized patterns. MCTS produces an optimized strategy <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}:S_{t}\to A_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}:S_{t}\to A_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}=\text{MCTS}(C,D)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>MCTS</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}=\text{MCTS}(C,D),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the initial strategy and <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> represents evaluation datasets. MCTS explores the strategy space by selecting nodes using the Upper Confidence Bound:</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UCB}(s)=\frac{w(s)}{n(s)}+c\sqrt{\frac{\ln N(s)}{n(s)}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>UCB</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UCB}(s)=\frac{w(s)}{n(s)}+c\sqrt{\frac{\ln N(s)}{n(s)}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(s)</annotation></semantics></math> is the cumulative reward, <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(s)</annotation></semantics></math> is the visit count, <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(s)</annotation></semantics></math> is the parent’s visit count, and <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is an exploration parameter. New strategy variants are generated via:</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(16)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{new}}=\text{SCG}(F_{t},P_{\text{modify}},C)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>new</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>SCG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>modify</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{new}}=\text{SCG}(F_{t},P_{\text{modify}},C),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.4" class="ltx_p">and evaluated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(17)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\text{Evaluate}(C_{\text{new}},D)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Evaluate</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>new</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\text{Evaluate}(C_{\text{new}},D).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Node statistics are updated as <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(s)=w(s)+R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(s)=w(s)+R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(s)=n(s)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(s)=n(s)+1</annotation></semantics></math>. By iteratively refining strategies based on real-time market inputs, MCTS enhances the robustness of <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and mitigates dependence on historical knowledge.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6. </span>Counterfactual Simulator</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Counterfactual Simulator (CS) is a pivotal component of FactFin, mitigating information leakage by testing strategies in counterfactual market environments. CS perturbs market data <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> within <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}=\{P_{t},F_{t},N_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}=\{P_{t},F_{t},N_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate alternative scenarios:</p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(18)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{cf}}=\text{Perturb}(D,\delta)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>cf</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Perturb</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{cf}}=\text{Perturb}(D,\delta),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{cf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>cf</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{cf}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the counterfactual dataset and <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> controls perturbation magnitude. Perturbations, such as modifying <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with noise <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> or adjusting <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, preserve statistical relationships. Strategy performance is assessed as:</p>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(19)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\phi(C,D),\quad R_{\text{cf}}=\phi(C,D_{\text{cf}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>cf</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>cf</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\phi(C,D),\quad R_{\text{cf}}=\phi(C,D_{\text{cf}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> evaluates strategy <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> on datasets <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{cf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>cf</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{cf}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Information leakage is quantified using Prediction Consistency (PC), Confidence Invariance (CI), and Input Dependency Score (IDS). Strategies with high PC and CI but low IDS rely on memorized patterns. CS optimizes strategies to minimize leakage:</p>
<table id="S3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(20)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}=\arg\min_{C}\{\alpha\cdot\text{PC}(C)+\beta\cdot\text{CI}(C)-\gamma\cdot\text{IDS}(C)\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>C</mi></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>PC</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>CI</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>IDS</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}=\arg\min_{C}\{\alpha\cdot\text{PC}(C)+\beta\cdot\text{CI}(C)-\gamma\cdot\text{IDS}(C)\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> are weights, ensuring <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is driven by real-time inputs and robust against leakage.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Workflow of Our FactFin</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            Market state <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}=\{P_{t},F_{t},N_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}=\{P_{t},F_{t},N_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, dataset <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>

